"""Final narrative additions; preserve the generator captured by refinement hashes."""
from pathlib import Path
src=Path(__file__).with_name('make_followup_report.py')
s=src.read_text()
old="p('Removing reaction is a diagnostic within the carriage. Its result does not by itself show superiority over the prescribed reference. Integrated error includes transients; peak load is reported alongside final accuracy. These comparisons should replace a broad claim that the ball mechanism is inherently better.')"
new="""p('In the stop–restart evaluation, carriage mean overshoot is 0.14 mm versus 0.99 mm for the prescribed reference and 2.62 mm with reaction removed. Carriage late error is 0.11 versus 0.33 mm, but integrated error increases from 10.12 to 13.03 mm s and settling occurs later (4.85 versus 4.66 s after engagement). This supports an overshoot–responsiveness tradeoff for these inherited gains. The single-seed pilot did not consistently favour the carriage, so the small evaluation cannot establish general superiority. Removing reaction isolates one mechanism component; it is not a tuned alternative controller.')"""
assert s.count(old)==1;s=s.replace(old,new)
old="p('Cell values are late error and completion counts. A large bias can produce a stable but incorrect endpoint; delay can worsen transients or destabilize correction. Recovery after a temporary missing progress stream differs from detecting physical contact loss. This limited grid supports task-specific guidance, not a universal EIT accuracy or bandwidth specification.')"
new="""p('With no added bias, all three trials pass at each tested additional delay through 500 ms. At zero added delay, either sign of 5 mm bias leaves only 1/3 passing, with mean late error about 5.1 mm. All progress-dropout conditions pass 3/3 after updates resume. Delay effects are nonmonotonic in this slow, finite-duration task; the results do not show that latency is beneficial or supply a maximum safe delay. Bias near the task tolerance is the clearer limitation. Contact detection remained available, so progress dropout does not test physical loss recovery.')"""
assert s.count(old)==1;s=s.replace(old,new)
needle="h('Recommended physical demonstration')"
s=s.replace(needle,"p('Halving the physics step to 0.5 ms in four representative feedback and attachment trials preserves all four outcomes. Maximum changes are 0.00493 mm in late error, 0.00243 degrees in peak pitch, and 0.00926 N in peak force. These checks support numerical consistency within the assumed model.')\n"+needle)
s=s.replace("SimpleDocTemplate(str(OUT/'Ball_Rolling_Followup_Report.pdf'),pagesize=(595,842)","SimpleDocTemplate(str(OUT/'Ball_Rolling_Followup_Report.pdf'),title='Ball rolling: what EIT adds',author='Simulation study',pagesize=(595,842)")
s=s.replace("'Prescribed reference, carriage, and carriage without reaction are tested at common physical observation times.','The results below determine the benefit; no general mechanism advantage is assumed.'", "'Stop–restart overshoot falls from 0.99 to 0.14 mm with the carriage, while integrated error rises from 10.12 to 13.03 mm s.','The tested carriage trades responsiveness for smaller overshoot; this is not a general superiority claim.'")
exec(compile(s,str(src),'exec'))
